### How to Boot a Secure, Full Visual Control Workload

To deploy a new microVM that manages and displays personal visual data within a minimal, memory-safe footprint, follow this process:

#### 1. Define the Identity & Visual Component (Frontend)
Your personal identity and security posture are visually broadcast via lightweight tactical CSS components embedded in the HTML [11, 12]. 

```html
<div class="terminal-window glow-emerald">
  <div class="bg-tactical-900" style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--t-border);">
    <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-400">PERSONAL IDENTITY & ATTESTATION</span>
  </div>
  <div style="padding: 1rem;">
    <div style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
      <span class="text-slate-400 text-xs uppercase block">ENVELOPE TYPE:</span>
      <span style="color: var(--cyan-400);">FROM_SCRATCH (32MB RAM)</span>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="text-slate-400 text-xs uppercase block">PUBLIC KEY HASH (ED25519):</span>
      <span class="text-xs" style="color: #94a3b8; word-break: break-all;">sha256:7c92...4f11</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```
*This design uses pure CSS variables and HTML to display your cryptographic key fingerprint and memory ceiling with zero bloat or dynamic framework vulnerabilities [12, 13].*

#### 2. Formulate the Secure Boot Contract (Contract Envelope)
In the interactive JSON editor inside `index.html`, declare the hardware and boot parameters targeting a FROM SCRATCH environment [12, 14, 15]:

```json
{
  "machine-config": {
    "vcpu_count": 1,
    "mem_size_mib": 32,
    "smt": false
  },
  "boot-source": {
    "kernel_image_path": "vmlinux-uncompressed",
    "boot_args": "console=ttyS0 reboot=k panic=1 pci=off init=/bin/init quiet"
  },
  "drives": [
    {
      "drive_id": "rootfs",
      "path_on_host": "rootfs.ext4",
      "is_root_device": true,
      "is_read_only": true
    }
  ]
}
```

#### 3. Build a FROM SCRATCH Root Filesystem
The guest workload runs with **zero operating system bloat**—no Ubuntu, Alpine, systemd, or glibc [14]. An empty 32MB ext4 image is created on the host, containing only a statically compiled musl Rust binary set as PID 1 (`/bin/init`) [14, 16]:

```bash
# 1. Create a blank 32MB filesystem image
dd if=/dev/zero of=rootfs.ext4 bs=1M count=32
mkfs.ext4 rootfs.ext4

# 2. Mount and build bare directory structure
mkdir -p /mnt/rootfs/{bin,dev,proc,sys,etc,tmp}

# 3. Copy your static musl Rust binary directly as PID 1
cp armybox-app /mnt/rootfs/bin/init
umount /mnt/rootfs
```

#### 4. Execute Hypervisor Instantiation
1. **Human-in-the-Loop Check:** The operator validates the JSON schema directly in the browser editor [17, 18].
2. **Dispatch:** Clicking "Deploy" issues a `POST` request (`fetch('/api/vm/start')`) containing the JSON contract to the host Rust backend [18, 19].
3. **Execution:** The Rust backend isolates the execution within the Firecracker Jailer sandbox, binds the virtual network interfaces, and maps the uncompressed kernel (`vmlinux-uncompressed`) directly into guest memory [19, 20]. 
4. **Boot Completion:** Because the kernel is uncompressed and the userland binary is the sole init process, the VM boots in **sub-5 milliseconds** with zero background daemons consuming RAM [16, 20, 21].

